  "response": "Determining whether a pressure vessel burst because of a flaw built into it at the shop or because of damage accumulated over years of service begins with the fracture surface itself, which preserves a durable record of the order in which the metal parted. The first task is therefore geometric rather than metallurgical: to locate the point of origin before any attempt is made to characterize the texture around it. That the fracture origin was located on the inner surface of a longitudinal seam weld is confirmed by the chevron marks on the fractured steel plate, which point back toward the fracture origin.\n\nReading that texture is the next step, and here the two parties' expectations diverge sharply. Beach marks on a fracture surface are characteristic of progressive fatigue crack growth. Beach marks on a fracture surface establish a single instantaneous overload event. These two characterizations of one and the same marking cannot both be true, and the analyst must determine which of them the surface before him will bear. Fatigue striations at a crack origin are resolved using scanning electron microscopy. Fatigue striations are visible to the unaided eye on a ruptured vessel wall. The beach-mark pattern at this origin shows that the crack grew incrementally over many pressure cycles; that is, the flaw extended in successive small steps under repeated loading.\n\nThe documentary record is then set against the metallurgical reading. The ruptured vessel was fabricated to ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code Section VIII. ASME Section VIII, Division 1 requires an explicit fatigue analysis for every vessel. Even so, fabrication to that Code did not prevent the seam weld from containing a lack-of-fusion flaw at its root, and the vessel was subjected to pressure cycles exceeding its design fatigue life; at least one of these two conditions \u2014 the unfused weld root or the cycling beyond design life, and perhaps both together \u2014 must obtain before a wall of this thickness will part under service pressure.\n\nThe unfused root is itself the decisive observation: a lack-of-fusion defect lying precisely at the initiation site confirms that the fracture origin contained a pre-existing weld flaw dating from fabrication. When the operator and the fabricator advance opposing accounts, the dispute reduces to one exclusive question of fact: either the fracture origin contained a pre-existing weld flaw dating from fabrication, or the fracture origin was free of any pre-existing weld flaw dating from fabrication, and while one of these must be the case, they cannot both be the case. Neither party's narrative can be accepted on assertion alone. The retained fabrication radiographs, the dimensional record of the initiating defect, and the plant's pressure-cycle log, reconciled against the measured crack-growth increments, determine which of the two alternatives the hardware supports, and that determination allocates responsibility without reference to what either party asserts.",
